A||N||11||86_Fastback||Water Pump||||1
Z||000000||86_Fastback||11-20-1999||04:30 PM||||I need to replace my water pump and will be putting on an underdrive pulley at the same time.  Can anyone recommend a replacement pump or tell me what to look for?  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||11-21-1999||09:09 PM||||When I replaced mine, I just bought a rebuilt water pump from NAPA (I think). Had to remove a plug from my original pump, and install it in the replacement. (The replacemant had a hose connection, instead of the plug.) Are you looking for details of the job? If so, let me know.||||reg||1
Z||000002||86_Fastback||11-21-1999||10:22 PM||||Raydar,<BR>I would appreciate any details you could give me on the procedure.  I don't think it will be too hard, just time consuming.  I was wondering if there was a heavy duty or high flow pump I should consider since I will be installing an overdrive pulley.<BR>-Gregg||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||11-22-1999||01:12 AM||||I have replaced two. They are pretty straight forward. But one I did had torx fasteners. Dont know why but what a pain.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DJRice||11-22-1999||08:41 AM||||The big deal with this job is making sure that you refill the cooling system properly after you replace the pump.  Make sure that you remove the cap from the Thermo Housing and the Radiator so that as you fill it the coolant will push all of the air out.||||reg||14
Z||000005||Raydar||11-22-1999||09:42 AM||||86... It's pretty straight-forward. Typical Fiero repair (you gotta take off 5 things to get to what you *really* want to remove.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)<BR>Remove the EGR solenoid, battery, etc. Mine had Torx fasteners, as well. The most difficult part of my repair was getting the bolts out of the waterpump pulley (it wants to turn), and getting all of the old gasket material off. Some of the bolts protrude into the water jackets. Should be relatively easy to tell which ones... Be sure to use waterproof sealer on those, when you reinstall them. I used pipe-joint compound, because it looked like that's what had been used, previously. Someone else may suggest something better. It's easier to fill the cooling system if the back of the car is on ramps, or jackstands. It'll take several attempts to get all of the air out. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||BENGAL4||11-22-1999||10:19 AM||||86,<P>I also  have an 86GT. I have replaced mine twce since Ive owned it. One thing I learned was that you need to remove the rear wheel and the inside skirting to gain easy access to all of the bolts.  Also, when you are removing the water pump pulley, leave the belt on, it makes it easier. When you remove the pump bolts, get a piece of styrofoam and put the the bolts there in the same configuration that you removed them in. This makes it easier to remember where they go. Finally,follow the filling recomendations given in the earlier posts. Good luck.<P>P.S.<P>I did not have torx bolts on mine, just a bunch of different sizes. I purchased a Metrinch tool kit from a TV infomercial and it was the best $125.00 that I have ever spent. They combine SAE and metrc into 1 size per socket or wrench. They also grab on the sides of the bolt, not the corners. Sears sells these now and I would highly recommend it. You don't waste alot of time searching for the right socket or wrench.<p>[This message has been edited by BENGAL4 (edited 11-22-1999).]||||reg||14
Z||000007||fieroshop||11-22-1999||10:19 AM||||One thing of major importance is that the water pump you replace does not have a plastic impeller.Plastic impelleers break down constantly and need to be replaced more often, the reason is that the water pump has to draw water from up front and it is okay in a front wheel drive but in a Fiero the radiator is too far away causing the impller to break and or spin on the shaft. I've had to replace quite a few for that reason including my own 88GT. The only way I can get a metal impeller is by buying remanufactured units.||||reg||11
Z||000008||86_Fastback||11-22-1999||05:57 PM||||Thanks for all the advice.  I was going to bring it to a dealership since I don't have much strength yet (had a heart valve replaced beginning of september.)  But, my brother has replaced a water pump on a 2.8L Buick Skylark and agreed to do the wrenching for me. So,I just picked up a reman. pump with a metal impellar.  Since I will have the coolant out, I decided to pick up all new hoses and clamps.  With 2 gallons of new coolant, sealant and some cleane, the grand total came to $68.<BR>-Gregg||||reg||1
Z||000009||BENGAL4||11-22-1999||10:09 PM||||When I replaced my first one, I was in a walking cast after rupturing my achilles tendon. I was running to first base and halfway there it blew. I went down like I was shot. Was in that walking cast for 14 weeks. It took me 3 days to replace the pump because I couldn't stand on it for long periods of time. Second time it took me 2 hours. ||||reg||14
Z||000010||86_Fastback||11-23-1999||03:20 PM||||My valve was bi-cuspid (2 flaps and not 3).  Had it since birth, but discovered when I was 16.  It was just time to replace it.  It takes 4-6 months for the breast bone and nerves to fully heal, so I have a little while before I can start working out to get my strength back to normal.||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||11-23-1999||08:04 PM||||Ouch.<P>get better soon dude.||||reg||1
